<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    FS.GG.Kit receiver (ADR-0062, .github#1262). This repo is a `coordination-kit` receiver: it holds
    the shared coordination skills, the `fsgg-coord` client, and the engine tool manifest on disk. It
    used to take them by byte-copy sync PR (`coordination-propagate`); it now REFERENCES the versioned
    `FS.GG.Kit` package and MATERIALIZES that kit onto disk from its pin. A hub change reaches this repo
    as an ordinary Renovate bump of the pin (in Directory.Packages.local.props), and `kit-materialize.yml`
    re-runs the materialize on that PR — the same shape lockfile-sync uses for packages.lock.json.

    This project exists ONLY to reference the package and drive its materialize. It compiles nothing and
    is deliberately NOT in FS.GG.SDD.sln, so the normal build never touches it. Materialize explicitly:

        dotnet build .config/kit/FS.GG.Kit.receiver.proj -t:FsggKitMaterialize

    The materialized files (skills, scripts/fsgg-coord, .config/dotnet-tools.json, and — because this is
    a `build-config` receiver — Directory.Build.props / Directory.Packages.props) are COMMITTED: the
    materialize is the write arm that replaces the byte-copy, not a live per-build input, so it does NOT
    run on every build (FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild=false, per the .github#1280 review). The committed bytes
    are content-addressed and byte-identical to canonical, so `coordination-coherence` stays green.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!-- A TFM is required by the SDK; nothing is compiled. Matches the repo's band (global.json). -->
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!-- Tooling-only: no source, no output, no lockfile of its own (the gate locks FS.GG.SDD.sln, not this). -->
    <EnableDefaultItems>false</EnableDefaultItems>
    <RestorePackagesWithLockFile>false</RestorePackagesWithLockFile>

    <!-- ADR-0067 §9 phase 4 (FS-GG/.github#1676, #1760 — the LAST of seven receivers). `.agents/skills`
         is a VIEW root now, not a materialized one: still in the runtime contract, still loaded by both
         runtimes, but its content is RESOLVED from `.claude/skills` at checkout by `scripts/skill-view
         generate` instead of being a second committed copy. THE RUNTIME ROOT SET IS UNCHANGED —
         ADR-0065 §A root's three dispositions makes it the UNION of these two properties, and that
         union is still ADR-0011's two roots as amended by ADR-0067 §5. Nothing is retired here; one
         copy stopped existing.

         MEASURED ON THIS TREE BEFORE THE RETIREMENT (f0e3d97), because the order requires it rather
         than allowing it to be inherited:

             $ diff -r .claude/skills .agents/skills
             Only in .claude/skills: skill-manifest.json

         That single asymmetry is DELIBERATE and is the whole subject of FS.GG.SDD#771: the
         producer-authoritative manifest lives in the TRACKED SOURCE root, where a view cannot delete
         it. The direction that matters for a retirement is the OTHER one — `Only in .agents/skills:`
         — and it is EMPTY. Hardened past the diff as well: 51 tracked paths under each root, identical
         relative path sets, identical git modes AND identical blob ids (`git ls-files -s` diffs to
         nothing), 0 symlinks on disk, 0 symlink index entries, and nothing untracked or ignored inside
         either root. Nothing lived only in the copy being retired, so nothing had to be relocated
         first and the retirement proceeded.

         AND `diff -r` IS NECESSARY BUT NOT SUFFICIENT (FS-GG/.github#1756). It compares the CONTENTS
         of two directories; it structurally cannot see a THIRD file, outside both roots, that names
         one of them as a path. FS.GG.Rendering's `diff -r` was silent and its
         `.template.config/template.json` still vendored the repo-root `.agents/skills/` into a
         shipped `dotnet new` template — `dotnet pack` did not fail, it silently shipped 1843 entries
         instead of 1914. So this repo's path CONSUMERS were searched too, not just its roots:
         `.fsproj` / `.props` / `.targets` / `.template.config` / packaging manifests / workflow YAML
         / scripts / test fixtures. Result, 2026-07-28:

           * SDD has NO `.template.config` outside `tests/fixtures/`, and NOTHING anywhere names
             `.agents/**` as a build, packaging or vendoring input.
           * It DOES hold one reference of exactly that shape, and it names the root that SURVIVES:
             `src/FS.GG.SDD.Commands/FS.GG.SDD.Commands.fsproj` embeds 16 seeded skill bodies as
             `<EmbeddedResource Include="../../.claude/skills/fs-gg-sdd-*/SKILL.md" />`. That is a
             second, independent reason `.claude/skills` had to be the live root here.
           * Measured rather than reasoned: `dotnet pack FS.GG.SDD.sln -c Release` on a bare checkout
             of the retired tree and on the pre-retirement tree produce the SAME 5 packages with the
             SAME 73 entries, entry-set identical but for NuGet's per-pack random `.psmdcp` name, and
             ZERO entries under `.agents` in either.
           * And the counterfactual is LOUD here, unlike Rendering's: repointing those 16 includes at
             `.agents/skills` on a bare checkout fails the build outright,
             `FSC error FS0078: Unable to find the file`. A LITERAL file path fails closed; a
             DIRECTORY or glob reference — Rendering's shape — fails silent. That distinction is the
             predictor worth having, and it is what #1756 should carry beside `diff -r`.

         Stage 2 of this phase refused to retire this repo partly BECAUSE that diff was not empty then:
         `skill-manifest.json` was at `.agents/skills/skill-manifest.json`, and a view would have
         deleted 6,891 tracked bytes with a completely clean `git status` (porcelain), after which this
         repo's required `gate` died at `producer manifest missing`. FS.GG.SDD#771 moved the file and
         its readers to `.claude/skills/`; that is why this retirement is possible at all.

         WHY `.claude/skills` IS THE LIVE ROOT AND NOT THE VIEW. The kit's four skill rows source
         `.claude/skills/<id>` in FS-GG/.github `registry/repos.yml`, and `registry/repos.lock`
         content-addresses those exact paths — so the materialize writes there, and
         `coordination-coherence` grades those bytes against this repo's pin. Making `.agents/skills`
         the live root instead would rename a `kit:` source, obliging a kit republish and a
         seven-receiver fan-out for a path rename. See FS-GG/.github
         `docs/coordination/skill-apparatus-retirement-order.md` §3.

         BOTH PROPERTIES ARE DECLARED EXPLICITLY, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING.
         `scripts/skill-view check` (with the `receiver-proj` flag) grades their union on this repo's required
         `skill-view-check` context and fails CLOSED on an absent property — an undeclared property
         would silently evaluate to the package default and hide exactly the regression that alarm
         exists for.

         `.codex/skills` IS NOT TOUCHED BY THIS CHANGE. It is the RETIRED root (ADR-0067 §5,
         FS-GG/.github#1636); the kit's retired-root sweep already withdrew the four kit-owned skills
         from it, and the 28 `fs-gg-sdd-*` / `speckit-*` / `spectre-console` skills remaining there are
         this repo's OWN. ADR-0065 §Retiring a root forbids anyone hand-deleting them, and this change
         does not: verified 28 before and 28 after. -->
    <FsggKitSkillRoots>.claude/skills</FsggKitSkillRoots>
    <FsggKitViewSkillRoots>.agents/skills</FsggKitViewSkillRoots>

    <!-- Materialize into the repo ROOT (two levels up from .config/kit/), on demand — never on build. -->
    <FsggKitReceiverRoot>$([System.IO.Path]::GetFullPath('$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../'))</FsggKitReceiverRoot>
    <FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild>false</FsggKitMaterializeOnBuild>

    <!-- This repo `receives: build-config`, so it also materializes Directory.Build.props /
         Directory.Packages.props to the repo root. First adoption of a hand-authored .props needs
         FsggKitAdoptBuildConfig=true for one run (.github#387); the canonical files here already carry
         the 'Source of truth: FS-GG/.github' marker, so no adopt is needed and the overwrite is free. -->
    <FsggKitMaterializeBuildConfig>true</FsggKitMaterializeBuildConfig>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!-- Version pinned centrally in Directory.Packages.local.props (Renovate-managed), like every other pin. -->
    <PackageReference Include="FS.GG.Kit" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    THE VIEW IS GENERATED BEFORE IT IS ASSERTED, IN EVERY TREE THE MATERIALIZE RUNS IN.

    `FsggKitCheckSkillView` (ADR-0067 §8, AfterTargets=FsggKitMaterialize) fails the build when a view
    root is absent, dangling, or partly visible — deliberately, because all three of those are exit 0
    with no diagnostic in BOTH runtimes. A view root is NOT committed (a committed symlink degrades to
    a plain text file under `git -c core.symlinks=false`, ADR-0067 §6), so EVERY fresh checkout starts
    without one: a clean clone, a new worktree, and a CI runner alike.

    MEASURED ON A BARE CHECKOUT OF THIS REPO'S RETIRED TREE, 2026-07-28, without this target:
    `dotnet build .config/kit/FS.GG.Kit.receiver.proj -t:FsggKitMaterialize` FAILS with
    "FS.GG.Kit: view skill root '.agents/skills' is ABSENT or a DANGLING link". That command is run by
    TWO jobs here, and only one of them is a file this repo can edit:

      * `gate.yml`'s `build-config-drift` job — the REQUIRED context "Shared-build-config drift
        check", which would have been red on every pull request; and
      * `kit-materialize.yml`, which is a `uses:` of FS-GG/.github's reusable workflow — a caller
        CANNOT add a step to it, so this repo's next Renovate FS.GG.Kit bump would red on a tree
        nobody touched, with no place to put a fix.

    The second is why the generate belongs HERE, next to the assertion, and not in a workflow step: a
    workflow step only covers the trees that run that workflow, and this repo does not own the workflow
    that needs it most. FS.GG.Templates reached this from stage 1's finding 1 and FS.GG.Governance
    re-measured it at stage 5 (FS-GG/.github#1748) naming FS.GG.SDD as owing the same; this is that
    debt paid, measured here rather than inherited.

    The tool is `scripts/skill-view` — THIS repo's copy, delivered and byte-verified by the FS.GG.Kit
    version this repo pins (a kit row since 0.15.0, together with `scripts/lib/args.sh` and
    `scripts/lib/roots.sh` which it sources at load), never one fetched from a moving hub `main`: a
    receiver's verdict must be a function of the receiver (FS-GG/.github#1584).

    The `roots` flag names the view roots EXPLICITLY rather than letting the tool fall back to
    ADR-0011's default pair. The fallback would also try to resolve `.claude/skills`, this repo's
    TRACKED live root, and `skill-view` refuses to generate over a tracked root with no override — so
    it would be a refusal rather than a no-op.

    The `Condition` guards on the PROPERTY, so if a later change empties FsggKitViewSkillRoots this
    target stops running with it instead of generating a view nothing asserts. That same emptying is
    what `scripts/skill-view check` (with the `receiver-proj` flag) reds on, and it is the ONLY thing that does: measured on
    this tree, with the property emptied and the root deleted, the materialize reports "no view skill
    roots declared — nothing to assert" and `coordination-sync` in its pinned check mode reports
    "OK — all 28 materialized file(s) match the FS.GG.Kit 0.15.0 this tree pins". Both green, the root
    simply gone from the runtime contract, and that second one is the REQUIRED context
    `kit / coordination-kit`.
  -->
  <Target Name="FsggSddGenerateSkillView" BeforeTargets="FsggKitCheckSkillView"
          Condition="'$(FsggKitViewSkillRoots)' != ''">
    <Exec WorkingDirectory="$(FsggKitReceiverRoot)"
          Command="bash scripts/skill-view generate --source $(FsggKitSkillRoots) --roots &quot;$(FsggKitViewSkillRoots.Replace(';',' '))&quot;" />
  </Target>

</Project>
